  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Sri Lanka Energy Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Sri Lanka):  As a result of electricity subsidies tariffs and due to a reliance on fossil fuel imports for power generation, Sri Lanka&apos;s energy system is inefficient and costly. Through the Sri Lanka Energy activity, USAID will provide technical assistance to the Ministry of Power and Energy, the Public Utilities Commission, the utility, the private sector, and other key sector counterparts to improve sector efficiency and sustainability. The activity has four key objectives: (1) Improve the cost-reflectivity of electricity tariffs (2) Increase the deployment of renewable energy (3) Improve demand side management and (4) Deploy advanced technologies.With FY 2022 funding to support government priorities to improve efficiency, USAID will support energy audits for supermarket chain outlets and financial institutions. In addition, USAID is also looking to pilot demand side management activities at a local-distribution level. To provide assistance to the Government of Sri Lanka to develop charging stations for smaller-scale two- or three- wheeled-vehicles, the activity will also conduct studies on financing mechanisms. This activity will also pilot initiatives that showcase storage solutions to ensure new renewable energy infrastructure is effective and conduct capacity-building for sector stakeholders to introduce new energy storage technologies. USAID continues to support the utility and regulator through the process of developing cost reflective tariffs; the development of an end user tariff model is an ongoing activity.The activity will accelerate sustainable energy independence and grid resilience in line with the Clean EDGE framework. The activity advances the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Objective 3.2: &apos;Assist Sri Lanka&apos;s move toward an equitable and clean energy future&apos;. The activity also supports Country Development Cooperation Strategy Development Objective 3: &apos;Resilience of resources to shocks and stresses enhanced&apos;.. .</narrative>
  </description>
